# release.scenario with uart's burst capped at 1 (M9; docs/design.md,
# "Transmit and receive"): the kernel command line's uart.burst=1, which
# root reads, says so in its summary and passes to uart in rdi at spawn, so
# uart writes one byte per THRE seen, though QEMU's 16550 has working
# FIFOs. uart's Stats then report burst 1 (MR8), and the run is otherwise
# release's: no kernel output, the probe's faults, the typed line read
# whole, and TEST PASS through isa-debug-exit. QEMU's transmitter takes
# each byte at once and raises THRE again, so one service routine empties
# the queue a byte at a time. The M9 Hyper-V check runs the same with
# burst 1 and 15 (tools/hyperv/m9-burst1.scenario, m9-burst15.scenario).
kernel: release
cmdline: test=release uart.burst=1
exit: success
expect: loader: microL4 loader
expect: entering kernel
next: root: microL4 root task
expect: root: cmdline "test=release uart.burst=1"
next: root: uart.burst=1: uart's burst capped at 1
expect: root: image frames: slots
expect: root: empty: slots
expect: release: watch (priority 255), uart (priority 200) with the COM1 IOPort and IRQ 4, console (priority 150); exit port 0xf4-0xf7
expect: release: a probe thread's DebugPutChar, DebugHalt, DebugDumpScheduler and reserved numbers 19 and 31 each raised UnknownSyscall with the number in MR18
expect: release: type a line
send: release input\r
expect: release: read "release input"
expect: release: uart:
expect: , 14 bytes received,
expect: , 0 overruns, 0 line errors,
expect: , burst 1, 0 stale THREs
expect: TEST PASS
forbid: microL4 kernel
forbid: bootinfo:
forbid: DEBUG HALT
forbid: KERNEL PANIC
forbid: ignored: not a number
